     "Their oldest daughter, Abigail, was preparing for examinations. Their younger son, Samuel, loved football and often found it difficult to sit still.",
     "One evening, Mr. Mensah announced that the family would begin reading the Bible together three times each week.",
     "Samuel immediately complained.",
     "“Father, do we have to study every evening? I already read at school.”",
     "Mr. Mensah smiled.",
     "“No, Samuel. We will not spend the entire evening studying. We will read a short passage, discuss it, pray, and learn how to apply it.”",
     "The first family meeting began with Deuteronomy 6:6–7.",
     "Esther explained that God’s Word was meant to become part of everyday family life.",
     "She asked Abigail, “What does it mean to teach God’s commandments diligently?”",
     "Abigail answered, “It means we should explain them carefully and help one another remember.”",
     "Mr. Mensah added, “And we must also practice what we teach.”",
     "During the following weeks, the family discussed honesty, kindness, forgiveness, obedience, and responsibility.",
     "When Samuel admitted that he had blamed another boy for breaking a football, the family discussed the importance of telling the truth.",
     "Instead of humiliating him, his parents helped him understand how dishonesty damages trust.",
     "Samuel apologized to his friend.",
     "Later, Abigail experienced anxiety about her examinations. Her mother reminded her that preparation was important, but her identity and worth did not depend on academic results.",
     "They prayed together and developed a study schedule.",
     "The family gradually discovered that Bible study was not merely a religious activity.",
     "It was helping them understand one another, make decisions, resolve conflicts, and grow in character.",
     "One evening, Samuel said, “I think I understand why we read the Bible together.”",
     "His father asked, “Why?”",
